Speak what a script says while the text to speech engine is still starting

Android's engine (Qt's qttexttospeech_android) binds to the system's text to speech service in the background: it
starts in the Error state, with ErrorReason::Initialization, and turns Ready once TextToSpeech's onInit arrives. A
ttsSpeak() before then - the first one a script makes, which also builds the engine - goes to
TextToSpeech.speak(), which answers "speak failed: not bound to TTS engine". The engine reports nothing new, being
in Error already, so the utterance is lost with no ttsSpeechError, ttsSpeechStarted or ttsSpeechReady.

- ttsSpeak() keeps the text while the engine is in Error for Initialization, the latest one replacing an earlier
  one as ttsSpeak() replaces what is being said; the engine's first Ready says it, and the events follow from the
  state changes as for any utterance.
- ttsSkip() drops a kept text.

The desktop's engines (speech-dispatcher, SAPI/WinRT, AVSpeechSynthesizer) are Ready once built, so nothing changes
there; one that cannot start at all stays silent, as before.

Tested by Mudlet/android's native/tests/android_smoke.py on API 28 and API 36 emulators with Google's engine: the
first ttsSpeak() raises ttsSpeechStarted. Without this, logcat has "speak failed: not bound to TTS engine" and no
event comes.

Carried by Mudlet/android's scripts/apply-mudlet-patches.sh until Mudlet/Mudlet has it.
